output:
{
  "title": "Three self-hosting bugs whose symptoms pointed somewhere other than their cause",
  "purpose": "A reference write-up for anyone self-hosting this project, so the next person who sees `unauthorized` on every call, a mangled `claude mcp add` line, or a silently ignored environment variable spends their time on the actual cause instead of rediscovering the diagnosis. Nothing here is being asked; this page collects no answers.",
  "resumeHint": "If you are hitting one of these on a fresh deployment, check the three items under 'What to verify on your own deployment' before reading any further into signatures, transports, or auth.",
  "blocks": [
    {
      "widget": "summary",
      "props": {
        "title": "Three things self-hosting exposed",
        "lead": "Running the project somewhere other than the original host surfaced three defects that had been invisible on the deployment that wrote them. Each announced itself with a symptom that pointed confidently at the wrong subsystem: an auth failure that was really an address failure, a shell command that was really a template-literal escape, and a config default that was really a parser giving up in silence. All three are fixed. The write-up is here because the misdirection is the expensive part, not the fix.",
        "points": [
          "`unauthorized` on every call was the client talking to the wrong host, not signing badly.",
          "A joined-up `claude mcp add` command was a line continuation eaten by a template literal, not a broken renderer.",
          "MAX_AWAIT_MS=90s becoming 90 seconds was an unparseable value falling back to a default without saying so.",
          "A fourth item — a CSS class collision — got a test rather than a fix, because it had already been found by eye twice."
        ]
      }
    },
    {
      "widget": "comparison",
      "props": {
        "title": "Symptom, where it points, where it actually was",
        "columns": [
          "Symptom",
          "What it reads as",
          "Actual cause"
        ],
        "rows": [
          [
            "`unauthorized` on every MCP call from a freshly installed client",
            "A broken or mismatched signature; key handling; clock skew",
            "The client had this deployment's URL hardcoded as its default base URL, so it signed correct requests and sent them to somebody else's host, which had never seen the key"
          ],
          [
            "The `claude mcp add` command on /start arrives as one long joined line",
            "A markdown or code-block renderer dropping newlines",
            "A lone trailing backslash inside a template literal is a line continuation, not a backslash — the source lines were joined and the continuation character silently eaten before rendering ever happened"
          ],
          [
            "MAX_AWAIT_MS=90s behaves as 90 seconds; awaits time out early or late",
            "A unit-parsing feature that exists and is being applied",
            "The value failed to parse as a number and the loader fell back to its default without reporting it, in a loader whose whole stated posture is refusing loudly"
          ]
        ],
        "caption": "In each row, the symptom was truthful and the inference from it was wrong. That is the pattern worth remembering more than any of the three fixes."
      }
    },
    {
      "widget": "findings",
      "props": {
        "title": "The three, in detail",
        "findings": [
          {
            "tone": "danger",
            "title": "The MCP client hardcoded one deployment as its default base URL",
            "detail": "A self-hoster who followed /start by hand installed a client whose default base URL was this deployment, not theirs. The client then signed requests with their key and sent them to a host that had never issued that key. Every call returned `unauthorized`, which reads as a signature problem, so the natural next move is to inspect key derivation, canonicalisation and clock skew — all of which are fine. The address was wrong. Fixed by having the instance substitute its own URL into the client file as it serves it, the way /cli already worked: a client downloaded from your deployment now talks to your deployment."
          },
          {
            "tone": "warn",
            "title": "A trailing backslash inside a template literal is a line continuation",
            "detail": "The multi-line `claude mcp add` command on /start was written in a JavaScript template literal with shell line continuations at the end of each line. In a template literal, a lone trailing backslash continues the source line: the newline and the backslash both disappear before the string exists. The rendered page therefore showed one joined line, with no trace of the continuation, and the page renderer — the obvious suspect — was innocent. Escape the backslash (`\\\\`) or build the command from an array and join it."
          },
          {
            "tone": "warn",
            "title": "An unparseable numeric env var fell back to its default in silence",
            "detail": "`MAX_AWAIT_MS=90s` looks like it means ninety seconds. It parsed to nothing, the loader substituted the default, and the process started normally. The behaviour that follows is a timeout that is neither what you configured nor obviously wrong — the worst kind, because the config file and the running system disagree with no evidence anywhere. This is in a loader whose stated posture is to refuse loudly rather than guess. It refuses now: an unparseable numeric value is a startup failure, not a shrug."
          },
          {
            "tone": "info",
            "title": "Fourth item: a stylesheet collision, fixed with a test rather than an eye",
            "detail": "The gallery page defined `.label`, which is also what the shared block renderer calls a field label. Every input inside a preview rendered uppercase and tiny. This class of bug has bitten twice and both times was caught by somebody noticing it, which is not a method. There is now a test that no page stylesheet redefines a class the shared stylesheet defines. It was proved to fail against the collision before being trusted — a guard that has never been seen to bite is an assertion about a guard, not a guard."
          }
        ]
      }
    },
    {
      "widget": "annotated-code",
      "props": {
        "title": "The two that are easiest to reproduce",
        "notes": [
          {
            "note": "The continuation bug. Inside a template literal, the backslash at the end of each line is consumed by JavaScript, not passed through to the reader as shell syntax. What the page shows is one line, and the backslashes are gone.",
            "language": "js",
            "code": "// Wrong: the newlines and backslashes vanish before the string exists\nconst cmd = `claude mcp add sirdocalot \\\n  --transport http \\\n  ${baseUrl}/mcp`;\n\n// Renders as:\n//   claude mcp add sirdocalot   --transport http   https://example/mcp\n\n// Right: escape the backslash, or don't use one\nconst cmd = [\n  'claude mcp add sirdocalot \\\\',\n  '  --transport http \\\\',\n  `  ${baseUrl}/mcp`,\n].join('\\n');"
          },
          {
            "note": "The silent-default bug. The old shape returns the default for any value that fails to parse, including values that were plainly meant to configure something. The new shape distinguishes 'absent' from 'present and nonsense'.",
            "language": "js",
            "code": "// Before: MAX_AWAIT_MS=90s -> NaN -> default, silently\nconst n = Number(process.env[key]);\nreturn Number.isFinite(n) ? n : fallback;\n\n// After: absent is fine, unparseable is fatal\nconst raw = process.env[key];\nif (raw === undefined || raw === '') return fallback;\nconst n = Number(raw);\nif (!Number.isFinite(n)) {\n  throw new Error(`${key}=${raw} is not a number (expected milliseconds)`);\n}\nreturn n;"
          }
        ]
      }
    },
    {
      "widget": "key-facts",
      "props": {
        "title": "What to verify on your own deployment",
        "facts": [
          {
            "key": "Client base URL",
            "value": "Open the MCP client file your instance serves and confirm the default base URL is your host, not the one you copied from. If it isn't, you downloaded a pre-fix client — fetch it again from your own deployment."
          },
          {
            "key": "The /start command",
            "value": "The `claude mcp add` block should appear as multiple lines with visible trailing backslashes. One long line means you are on a pre-fix build; the command will still work if you paste it, but read it before trusting it."
          },
          {
            "key": "Numeric environment variables",
            "value": "Values are milliseconds and must parse as bare numbers. `90s`, `1_000` and `90 ms` are now startup failures rather than silent defaults. If your instance suddenly refuses to boot after an upgrade, this is the likely reason and the error names the variable."
          },
          {
            "key": "Page stylesheets",
            "value": "Do not define a class the shared stylesheet already defines — `.label` in particular. The test suite now fails on collisions rather than waiting for someone to notice tiny uppercase text."
          }
        ]
      }
    },
    {
      "kind": "callout",
      "tone": "info",
      "text": "The common thread: all three defects were invisible on the deployment that authored them, because that deployment was the hardcoded default, rendered its own /start page, and had a valid config file. Self-hosting is not only a feature here; it is the test environment that makes assumptions about 'the host' fail loudly enough to find."
    }
  ]
}
